<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Troad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Biga Peninsula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Troad is the historical name of the Biga Peninsula in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, bounded by the Dardanelles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>. It was the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, and from the early first millennium BCE Aeolian Greek cities such as Mytilene founded settlements on its coast. It later belonged to the Persian, Hellenistic, Roman and Ottoman empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troad.jpg" alt="Troad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CherryX · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walls_of_Troy_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
